r stdout), supports append mode with the -a flag, handles signals properly, and returns correct exit codes. This is maybe 80-150 lines of Go, so relatively simple. Given the constraint of one task slot and the preference for implementation before tests, I should propose just the implementation task rather than splitting it.\n\nBut I'm running into a problem with the constraints—they require anchoring tasks to specific PRD requirement IDs like prd001 R1.1, and without knowing the exact PRD structure, I can't cite those properly. I need to figure out how to reference the requirements generically while still meeting that constraint, or look at how the golden example handled this situation. I need to figure out the PRD file naming pattern without using tools. Looking at what I know—magefiles is prd011, with true and false as orphaned prd012 and prd013—I can work backward to estimate where sponge falls in the sequence. Rather than guess a specific number, I should just reference the sponge PRD by its likely location and let the agent locate it, or pick a reasonable number based on the component ordering I've observed. I'm specifying the required reading materials—the sponge PRD, use case documentation, test suite, architecture guidelines, and engineering standards—then laying out the files to create for the main implementation and its tests.\n\nBut wait, the project guidelines say to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, so I should focus just on the implementation task here. The acceptance criteria can still verify the code compiles and produces the expected output without requiring tests to pass in this particular task.\n\nNow I'm laying out the specific requirements for the sponge implementation: reading stdin into a buffer, writing to a file, and supporting an append flag.\n\nI need to follow the existing cmd/ package structure and use standard Go libraries for file I/O, then verify the implementation works correctly with basic integration tests like piping input and checking file output. I'm realizing I can't invent requirement IDs without seeing the actual PRD content, and since I can't use tools to access it, I need to reference the PRD directly and let the agent read the specific requirements themselves rather than guessing at the structure. The example shows I can anchor tasks to PRD references like \"prd003-crumbs-interface,\" so I should do the same here—reference the sponge PRD and have the agent implement based on what it finds there.\n\nOn the testing question, I'm constrained to a single task and the pattern in other utilities shows both implementation and tests live in the same directory, but the planning constitution says to split them. Since I only have one task slot, I'll focus on implementation now and leave tests for a future session.\n\nLooking at the code status, sponge is spec_complete but code hasn't started yet. I'll write the task to reference the PRD directly so the agent can pull the exact requirements, and keep my acceptance criteria focused on compilation and basic functional verification rather than full test coverage.\n\nFor the file naming, main.go is correct for cmd/ packages even though the general convention is to name files after exported types. I'm also mapping out the four core requirements that fit the constraint: stdin buffering, file output, append mode flag, and error handling with proper exit codes. The test suite reference would be test-rel-01.2.yaml once I finalize the task.\n\nI need to be careful about only including design decisions that come directly from the architecture document—the temp file strategy for buffering, the cmd/ package structure with main.go, and the pkg/testutils/ differential testing harness—rather than inventing anything new.\n\nNow I'm wrapping up with a final summary of the project state: the problem we're solving (formal specs for Unix utilities with AI-generated Go implementations verified through differential testing), the four-stage pipeline architecture with its key components across cmd/, pkg/, docs/, and build infrastructure, and where release 01.2 stands—specs are complete but the generated code hasn't made it to the working branch yet.\n\nFor the immediate priority, sponge is the earliest release with a finished spec and test suite but no implementation. It's a straightforward utility that buffers stdin before writing to a file, with an append flag as its main feature, so I'm proposing we tackle the Go implementation as the next task.\n\nThe implementation doesn't have external dependencies beyond the standard library, though it will eventually need differential testing against the Homebrew reference using the existing testutils package. I'm aware this creates a gap—without tests I can't verify output parity—but given the one-task constraint and the principle that implementation precedes tests, I'll focus on getting the code written first.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19056,"cache_read_input_tokens":16066,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19056,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ccb6f2d-b998-492b-9196-830247dc447a","uuid":"b3ed65f3-052b-4fa3-a247-22edd4a09340"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01PxFUhev72uCxnrR7z5wCB6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications accessible to implementers. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Components: `cmd/` (one Go package per utility), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `magefiles/` (build targets), `docs/` (specs, PRDs, use cases, test suites).\n\n**Current state:** Specs are written for multiple utilities. Generated code exists at generation-merged tags (pkg/testutils, cmd/ts, cmd/cat, cmd/wc, cmd/sponge, pkg/sys, pkg/format) but not on the working branch. The working branch has specifications only.\n\n**Current release:** Release 01.2 — sponge. Use case `rel01.2-uc001-sponge` is spec_complete (PRD, use case, and test suite exist), code not started.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nRelease 01.2 is the target. Sponge is a moreutils tool that reads all of stdin into a buffer before writing to a file, enabling patterns like `sort file | sponge file`. It has one primary flag (`-a` for append). This is a simple utility — the implementation task fits within the line budget. Per P2, tests are split into a separate task (future session). Per P8, implementation comes before tests.\n\nSponge has no code dependencies beyond the Go standard library (no pkg/sys or pkg/format needed). The implementation is a single `cmd/sponge/main.go` file.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/sponge -- sponge PRD core requirements (stdin buffering, file write, append mode)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (find and read the sponge PRD -- file matching prd*-sponge.yaml)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.2-uc001-sponge.yaml (sponge use case -- end-to-end execution path)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-01.2.yaml (sponge test suite -- acceptance reference for expected behavior)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component conventions, design decisions DD2 and DD3)\n      - docs/engineering/ (read all engineering guidelines for code conventions and patterns)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/sponge/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: sponge utility implementation -- stdin buffering, file write, -a append flag, exit codes\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: Read all of stdin into a temporary file before writing output, per the sponge PRD requirement for stdin buffering (sponge must consume all input before any write to prevent data loss when reading and writing the same file)\n      - id: R2\n        text: Write buffered content to the file path specified as the positional command-line argument, per the sponge PRD; if no file argument is provided, write to stdout\n      - id: R3\n        text: Support the -a flag for appending to the output file instead of truncating, per the sponge PRD append-mode requirement\n      - id: R4\n        text: Implement exit codes and error handling per the sponge PRD -- exit 0 on success, exit 1 on errors (file open failure, write failure, stdin read failure), write error messages to stderr\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: Follow cmd/ package conventions from ARCHITECTURE.yaml -- one package per utility under cmd/sponge/, main.go as the implementation file, package main with func main()\n      - id: D2\n        text: Use a temporary file in the same directory as the target file for atomic write safety -- write stdin to temp file first, then rename or copy to target; this prevents partial writes on interruption\n      - id: D3\n        text: Use Go standard library only (os, io, flag, fmt) -- sponge does not require pkg/sys or pkg/format; keep the import footprint minimal per ARCHITECTURE.yaml technology choices\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/sponge/main.go compiles without errors via go build ./cmd/sponge/\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Binary soaks up all stdin before writing: printf 'hello\\\\nworld' | ./bin/sponge /tmp/sponge_test writes 'hello\\\\nworld' to /tmp/sponge_test\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Binary supports -a flag: running sponge -a on a file with existing content appends rather than truncates\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Binary writes to stdout when no file argument is given: echo test | ./bin/sponge prints test to stdout\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: Binary exits 1 and prints an error to stderr when the output file path is not writable\n      - id: AC6\n        text: go vet ./cmd/sponge/ reports no issues\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19056,"cache_read_input_tokens":16066,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19056,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ccb6f2d-b998-492b-9196-830247dc447a","uuid":"073d06f2-edbc-4c90-8fd6-3f9755c6e529"}
